ms I have a good start. I can try to continue towards a winning hand. W7 is the orphan.\nThe unshown list says W7:4. If the list includes my hand of W7:2, only 2 left. The total of each tile in the game is 4.\n\nI can discard W7 to keep T2-T4 and B2-B4. Or discard T2 and W7 pair.\nHere is the important part: Let me see if the tiles can be optimized for Yaku for a winning hand with more points.\n\nW3-W4-W5, W6-W7-W8, and T5-T5-T5. I have three completed. I am 2-shanten. I need one more completed meld and one pair. I am 1-shanten. Potential pairs are: W7, T2-T4, B2-B4. Or, I can discard any of the incomplete and wait for the perfect tile!\n\nIf the hand is structured to have those 3 sets. W7, T2, T4, B2, B4 makes me 1-shanten.\n\nI can make a strategy to aim for a Closed Hand, and self-draw. Is this the path I need? This is a great 1-shanten move! Closed wait. Self drawn with this structure. Let's aim to see if that works.\n\nCurrently, if I focus on All Simples, it gives me a base score of 2 points. If I can complete the hand using simple tiles, I can also add the yaku \"No Honors\". If I draw the matching tile, I also have \"Closed Wait\" (Wait tiles) and \"Self-drawn\". This might give me a good path!\n\nThis gives 8 points total, which allows a win. I also check for 8-points with self-drawn yaku. I can also play for the Mixed Double Chow pattern! Let's check: Tanyao. Pung of Terminals/Honors? Short Straight? Pure Straight? If I draw B3 or T3, I remain\n\n", "output": "The current hand consists of the following sets and tiles:\n*   Complete Chow: [W3, W4, W5]\n*   Complete Chow: [W6, W7, W8]\n*   Pung: [T5, T5, T5]\n*   Isolated/Incomplete: W7 (since the other W7 is used in the Chow), T2, T4, B2, B4.\n\nThe hand has 5 blocks formed or forming:\n1.  [W3, W4, W5]\n2.  [W6, W7, W8]\n3.  [T5, T5, T5]\n4.  [T2, T4] (Waiting for T3)\n5.  [B2, B4] (Waiting for B3)\nPlus a single [W7].\n\nTo win, the hand needs 4 sets and 1 pair. Currently, there is no pair (unless we break a set or treat W7 as a potential pair if we draw another). The extra W7 is effectively an orphan tile.\n\nThere are two main strategies:\n\n**Strategy A: All Simples + One Voided Suit (Aiming for 1-shanten)**\n*   Discard B2 and B4 to go for **One Voided Suit** (1 point).\n*   Hand structure: [W3, W4, W5], [W6, W7, W8], [T5, T5, T5], [T2, T4] + Pair [W7] (if another drawn) or similar.\n*   This path is fast (1-shanten if we consider the Bamboos dead), but scoring is tricky.\n*   Yaku: All Simples (2) + One Voided Suit (1) + No Honors (1) + Fully Concealed (4) = 8 points.\n*   **Problem:** Fully Concealed requires Tsumo (Self Draw) or winning on a concealed hand (2 pts) + Ron which sums to only 6-7 points. Relying solely on Tsumo is risky.\n\n**Strategy B: Mixed Shifted Chows (Aiming for 8 points, Ron allowed)**\n*   Target structure: [B2, B3, B4], [W3, W4, W5], [T4, T5, T6], [W6, W7, W8] + Pair [T5].\n*   To achieve this:\n    *   Keep [B2, B4] and wait for B3.\n    *   Keep [W3, W4, W5] and [W6, W7, W8].\n    *   Convert [T5, T5, T5] + [T4] into Chow [T4, T5, T6] + Pair [T5]. This requires drawing **T6**.\n    *   This forms the **Mixed Shifted Chows** pattern (B234, W345, T456) worth 6 points.\n    *   Plus **All Simples** (2 points) = 8 points.\n*   This hand is valid for winning on Ron or Tsumo.\n*   The hand is currently 2-shanten for this pattern (Needs B3 and T6; Needs to discard W7 and T2).\n*   Tiles availability: T6 (4 left), B3 (2 left).\n\nComparing the two, Strategy B offers a guaranteed valid winning hand (8 points) regardless of whether the winning tile is self-drawn or discarded by others. Strategy A risks being unable to declare a win on a discard.\nTherefore, moving towards Mixed Shifted Chows is the superior choice. This requires discarding the useless tiles W7 and T2. T2 is safer (2 visible), while W7 is less visible, but W7 is a central tile. In terms of hand building for this specific pattern, both are useless. We choose to discard W7 first as it is an isolated duplicate of a tile effectively used in a sequence, and to keep T2 temporarily as a safe discard for a later turn.\n\nEvaluation: The hand is best shaped towards \"Mixed Shifted Chows\" (6 pts) + \"All Simples\" (2 pts) for a total of 8 points. This requires forming B234, W345, and T456 sequences. The current W7 is redundant (one used in W6-7-8, one isolated) and does not contribute to this pattern. Discarding W7 advances the hand towards the goal.\nReason: The isolated W7 is not useful for the target 8-point hand (Mixed Shifted Chows). Although the hand becomes 2-shanten, it guarantees the minimum points required to win on Ron, whereas the faster alternative (One Voided Suit) leaves the hand short of points unless self-drawn.\nAnswer: Play W7"}
